<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domingo de Monteverde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1773–1832</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Domingo de Monteverde was a Spanish naval officer born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> who led the royalist reconquest of Venezuela in 1812. Advancing from Coro after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> earthquake, he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> to capitulate at San Mateo in July 1812, ending the First Republic of Venezuela, and ruled harshly as captain-general. He was driven back by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admirable-campaign">Admirable Campaign</a> of 1813 and left Venezuela after being wounded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domingo-de-monteverde.jpg" alt="Domingo de Monteverde" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domingo-de-Monteverde.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
